## Adding and Removing from the End

`.push()` adds to the end of a Vec. `.pop()` removes from the end and returns the removed item — wrapped in an `Option`.

### Push: Add to the End

```xiom
let stack: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.push(30);
```

The Vec now contains `[10, 20, 30]` — in that exact order.

### Pop: Remove from the End

```xiom
let top = stack.pop();
```

`.pop()` removes the LAST item and returns it. But what if the Vec is empty? There's nothing to remove — so `.pop()` returns `Option[T]`:

- `Some(value)` — when there was an item to pop
- `None` — when the Vec was empty

### Matching on the Result

```xiom
match stack.pop() {
  Some(val) -> io.println(val);
  None -> io.println("Stack is empty!");
}
```

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let stack: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  stack.push(100);
  stack.push(200);

  match stack.pop() {
    Some(v) -> io.println(v);
    None -> io.println("empty");
  }

  match stack.pop() {
    Some(v) -> io.println(v);
    None -> io.println("empty");
  }

  match stack.pop() {
    Some(v) -> io.println(v);
    None -> io.println("empty");
  }

  return 0;
}
```

This prints `200`, `100`, then `"empty"` — because after two pops, the stack is empty and `.pop()` returns `None`.

